00:18:04,401 --> 00:18:06,280
it took man well beyond the boundaries

251
00:18:06,281 --> 00:18:07,620
that man had ever explored.

252
00:18:07,621 --> 00:18:09,540
It took man into the universe,

253
00:18:09,541 --> 00:18:13,960
and it took man inside of his own body.

254
00:18:13,961 --> 00:18:15,780
With a simple microscope, which is all it is,

255
00:18:15,781 --> 00:18:17,620
is a bead of glass,

256
00:18:17,621 --> 00:18:22,120
discovered bacteria, and with, in effect, two eyeglass lenses

257
00:18:22,121 --> 00:18:26,220
held at a distance apart, Galileo created a telescope

258
00:18:26,221 --> 00:18:29,660
and studied the emotions of the planets and the surface of the

259
00:18:29,661 --> 00:18:33,660
moon. Glass helped us realize that

260
00:18:33,661 --> 00:18:37,140
not only were we not the center of the universe, we weren't even

261
00:18:37,141 --> 00:18:40,980
that significant a part of it. Nevertheless, this did not stop

262
00:18:40,981 --> 00:18:45,020
us from gazing at ourselves in a more reflective mirror, perfected

263
00:18:45,021 --> 00:18:49,260
perfected in the late 1600s. Glass is interesting in that

264
00:18:49,261 --> 00:18:52,620
when you put a coating on it, it's kind of a doppelganger.

265
00:18:52,621 --> 00:18:57,500
It takes on an opposite personality, so you put one

266
00:18:57,501 --> 00:19:00,700
coating on it and the transparent thing you know so

267
00:19:00,701 --> 00:19:06,700
well instantly becomes reflective. As scientists

268
00:19:06,701 --> 00:19:10,060
expanded our knowledge with glass lenses, others were making

269
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:13,740
the world a safer place with them. Early lighthouses were

270
00:19:13,741 --> 00:19:17,240
simply fires on cliffs or pedestals, subject to wind and

271
00:19:17,241 --> 00:19:21,240
rain. Glass allowed keepers to protect

272
00:19:21,241 --> 00:19:25,080
the light without concealing it.

273
00:19:25,081 --> 00:19:28,180
Keepers soon realized that by putting a mirror bent into a

274
00:19:28,181 --> 00:19:32,200
parabola behind the light, the mirror reflected and increased

275
00:19:32,201 --> 00:19:35,200
the intensity, or candle power, of the light.

276
00:19:39,840 --> 00:19:43,200
Before long, specially designed Fresnel lenses increased

277
00:19:43,201 --> 00:19:48,000
candle power by many thousands. As the many faceted glass drum

278
00:19:48,001 --> 00:19:51,840
was set down around the illuminant, the circular lens

279
00:19:51,841 --> 00:19:56,640
captured light and redirected it into a single, powerful beam.

280
00:19:56,641 --> 00:20:00,240
Glass now projected information, communicating through light

281
00:20:00,241 --> 00:20:02,160
across miles.

282
00:20:05,760 --> 00:20:09,760
But these specialized uses for glass belie its ubiquity.

283
00:20:09,761 --> 00:20:13,560
During the 19th century, the versatility of glass collided

284
00:20:13,561 --> 00:20:17,800
with the Industrial Revolution. It soon became clear that the

285
00:20:17,801 --> 00:20:21,120
modern world could not make its way without glass, and lots of

286
00:20:21,121 --> 00:20:25,120
it. The Crystal Palace, built for the

287
00:20:25,121 --> 00:20:29,180
first World's Fair in London's Hyde Park in 1851, required over

288
00:20:29,181 --> 00:20:33,560
300,000 panes of glass. Glass will return on Modern

289
00:20:33,561 --> 00:20:34,261
Marvels.

290
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:44,460
We now return to Glass on Modern Marvels.

291
00:20:44,461 --> 00:20:48,560
Utility and artistry are married at Waterford in Ireland, where

292
00:20:48,561 --> 00:20:52,060
skilled craftsmen work leaded crystal glass into sparkling

293
00:20:52,061 --> 00:21:00,260
goblets and vases by hand. In 1674, English glassmaker

294
00:21:00,261 --> 00:21:04,060
George Ravenscroft patented a method of making fine crystal

295
00:21:04,061 --> 00:21:08,160
using lead oxide instead of potash.

296
00:21:08,161 --> 00:21:11,820
True crystal must contain at least 30% lead by weight,

297
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:15,320
although some fine crystals contain over 50%.

298
00:21:15,321 --> 00:21:20,060
The result is a brilliant glass that is strong enough to be deeply cut.

299
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:24,680
Lead also lowers the temperature at which glass softens,

300
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:28,380
increasing its pliancy. But that's not all it does.

301
00:21:29,100 --> 00:21:31,620
It increases the refractive index.

302
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:37,880
And what it means is that once you cut the piece of leaded glass,

303
00:21:37,881 --> 00:21:39,460
in two specific shapes.

304
00:21:39,760 --> 00:21:44,660
It reflects glass in very magic ways in many different directions.

305
00:21:45,500 --> 00:21:47,720
For an optician, it's actually a problem.

306
00:21:47,820 --> 00:21:50,240
They would call it chromatic aberration, those rainbows that you see.

307
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:53,240
But for a glass designer, it's a dream come true.

308
00:21:55,340 --> 00:21:59,180
Crystal itself seems like an aberration in the history of glass production.

309
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:02,440
The labor-intensive process was being refined,

310
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:06,220
just as mass production revolutionized the world of glass.

311
00:22:06,221 --> 00:22:10,220
technology replaced old pot furnaces with tank furnaces that

312
00:22:10,221 --> 00:22:14,220
greatly increased glass output. One effect of these larger

313
00:22:14,221 --> 00:22:18,220
furnaces was a better way to produce sheet glass for windows.

314
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:26,220
The old rondelle and cylinder method for making flat glass had

315
00:22:26,221 --> 00:22:30,220
held sway for centuries. But in 1905, Emile Foucault was

316
00:22:30,221 --> 00:22:34,220
able to pull up vertically from a tank furnace a continuous and

317
00:22:34,221 --> 00:22:39,220
and uniform sheet of glass. It involved dipping a bait into a

318
00:22:39,221 --> 00:22:43,220
pot of molten glass and drawing it up as if you would put a knife

319
00:22:43,221 --> 00:22:47,220
in a pan of honey and lift the knife up and the honey would stick

320
00:22:47,221 --> 00:22:51,220
to the knife. Except this honey is over 2,000 degrees and the

321
00:22:51,221 --> 00:22:54,220
knife you just put in there is likely to start degrading

322
00:22:54,221 --> 00:22:58,220
rapidly because hot glass is incredibly corrosive.

323
00:22:58,221 --> 00:23:01,220
The Furco process lifts up the glass and sends it through a

324
00:23:01,221 --> 00:23:04,720
through a series of rollers, producing a flat but relatively

325
00:23:04,721 --> 00:23:08,120
rippled and distorted piece of glass.

326
00:23:08,121 --> 00:23:11,360
Through grinding and polishing, the glass is smoothed, though

327
00:23:11,361 --> 00:23:14,940
for every ton of sheet glass produced, another ton is thrown

328
00:23:14,941 --> 00:23:18,880
back into the tank. The Fourco process was greatly

329
00:23:18,881 --> 00:23:22,000
improved by Alistair Pilkington of the Pilkington Brothers

330
00:23:22,001 --> 00:23:25,920
flat glass manufacturers. He theorized that if you

331
00:23:25,921 --> 00:23:31,220
poured molten glass onto a pool of liquid metal, it would be

332
00:23:31,221 --> 00:23:35,660
that the glass would float on that surface of liquid metal.

333
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:39,160
The bottom surface that touched the liquid metal would be smooth

334
00:23:39,260 --> 00:23:42,960
and the top surface would be smoothed by the forces of gravity.

335
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:47,460
Pilkington chose molten tin and after seven years of trying,

336
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:50,480
finally produced a smooth sheet of glass.

337
00:23:50,580 --> 00:23:54,480
The float process was introduced in 1959, surprisingly late in

338
00:23:54,580 --> 00:23:58,480
the chronology of flat glass manufacture, but just in time to

339
00:23:58,481 --> 00:24:01,280
to help address the increase in demand for windows

340
00:24:01,380 --> 00:24:05,080
after the post-World War II economic boom.

341
00:24:05,180 --> 00:24:07,720
Polkington claims to have the world record

342
00:24:07,820 --> 00:24:12,120
for making bad glass before they ever made a good sheet.

343
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:14,860
It's notable in that the great breakthroughs

344
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:17,600
in glass technology often come at the tail end

345
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:21,700
of an extremely long and costly struggle with a process

346
00:24:21,800 --> 00:24:25,540
that no one actually knows will ever produce anything good.

347
00:24:25,640 --> 00:24:27,580
And that's the story of optical fiber.

348
00:24:27,581 --> 00:24:31,420
fiber, that's the story of float glass, that's the story of the

349
00:24:31,421 --> 00:24:35,740
creation of the light bulb. Speaking of light bulbs, when

350
00:24:35,741 --> 00:24:39,020
Thomas Edison was experimenting with electric light, he went to

351
00:24:39,021 --> 00:24:42,140
the Corning Company to see if they could blow glass bulbs,

352
00:24:42,141 --> 00:24:47,020
inside which his filaments would glow. Without glass, it is not

353
00:24:47,021 --> 00:24:52,780
possible to have light without fire. And so if you think of the

354
00:24:52,781 --> 00:24:55,820
world in which we live and all the ways we create light,

355
00:24:55,821 --> 00:25:00,120
as being open flames without glass being the tool to make

356
00:25:00,121 --> 00:25:02,620
it work. Light bulbs need to be

357
00:25:02,621 --> 00:25:05,820
relatively thin, yet strong enough to withstand the

358
00:25:05,821 --> 00:25:10,060
hermetic vacuum the electric filament needs in order to glow.

359
00:25:10,061 --> 00:25:13,980
Edison's prototype light bulbs were blown one at a time and

360
00:25:13,981 --> 00:25:18,380
were not particularly strong. At Corning, Edison enlisted

361
00:25:18,381 --> 00:25:21,520
experienced glass blowers who were better able to produce the

362
00:25:21,521 --> 00:25:24,520
thin-walled bulbs.

363
00:25:24,521 --> 00:25:38,521
Here, Corning glassblowers produce a giant light bulb that

364
00:25:40,580 --> 00:25:43,580
might be used for a lighthouse or some other specialized

365
00:25:43,581 --> 00:25:48,580
purpose. One of those glassblowers is

366
00:25:48,581 --> 00:25:52,580
a fellow named Billy Woods, who grows up in a glass factory

367
00:25:52,581 --> 00:25:56,580
blowing light bulbs by hand. Woods, like all glassmakers,

368
00:25:56,680 --> 00:26:00,380
is fascinated by the molten material.

369
00:26:00,480 --> 00:26:05,360
And the story goes that he was working on a different glass

370
00:26:05,460 --> 00:26:07,360
making process that produced a continuous stream

371
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:11,260
of molten glass. Woods, then a manager at Corning,

372
00:26:11,360 --> 00:26:14,300
picked up a shovel with a hole in it and stuck it

373
00:26:14,400 --> 00:26:18,480
into the stream of glass. As the molten glass poured

374
00:26:18,580 --> 00:26:21,440
through the shovel, it resembled the ball of glass

375
00:26:21,441 --> 00:26:25,940
or gob produced by a glass blower's initial blow.

376
00:26:25,941 --> 00:26:27,940
Woods was struck with an idea.

377
00:26:27,941 --> 00:26:30,940
He envisioned a machine that would pull a ribbon of glass

378
00:26:30,941 --> 00:26:34,940
through an opening, drape it over a mold, puff air into it,

379
00:26:34,941 --> 00:26:37,940
and automatically create a perfect uniform bulb.

380
00:26:40,440 --> 00:26:42,940
He then proceeded to construct one of the most ingenious

381
00:26:42,941 --> 00:26:46,940
inventions in the history of glass production, the ribbon

382
00:26:46,941 --> 00:26:51,440
machine, introduced in 1922 and little altered to the history

383
00:26:51,441 --> 00:26:56,240
This whole contraption is moving so fast

384
00:26:56,340 --> 00:26:59,340
that when you watch it working, you cannot actually see

385
00:26:59,440 --> 00:27:01,580
the individual bulbs being made.

386
00:27:03,780 --> 00:27:06,580
It is making light bulbs faster than a machine gun

387
00:27:06,680 --> 00:27:08,500
can fire bullets.

388
00:27:08,600 --> 00:27:11,900
A ribbon machine is so fast and so efficient

389
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:15,840
that 16 of them can make the entire world's production

390
00:27:15,940 --> 00:27:16,740
of light bulbs.

391
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:31,440
The fastest ribbon machines make over 2,000 bulbs a minute.

392
00:27:31,441 --> 00:27:34,440
Soon copper wire brought electricity into homes and

393
00:27:34,441 --> 00:27:36,440
businesses. Again, glass was

394
00:27:36,441 --> 00:27:39,440
indispensable. Exposed wire connections were

395
00:27:39,441 --> 00:27:42,440
protected from arcing by bell-shaped insulators, often

396
00:27:42,441 --> 00:27:46,440
made of glass because of its poor electrical conductivity and

397
00:27:46,441 --> 00:27:51,440
its imperviousness to water. As Edison was tinkering

398
00:27:51,441 --> 00:27:54,740
his light bulb, an inventor named Michael Owens endeavored

399
00:27:54,741 --> 00:27:57,840
to create an automatic bottle-making machine.

400
00:27:57,841 --> 00:28:02,380
After seeing Coca-Cola at the Columbian Exposition in 1893,

401
00:28:02,381 --> 00:28:06,280
Owens knew that the recently invented crown cap could seal

402
00:28:06,281 --> 00:28:09,100
beverages. But those two things needed a

403
00:28:09,101 --> 00:28:14,500
bottle with a uniform neck that would make it all work.

404
00:28:14,501 --> 00:28:17,800
Owens clearly understood the need to make a bottle by

405
00:28:17,801 --> 00:28:20,640
machine. He also understood the social

406
00:28:20,641 --> 00:28:24,380
to make a bottle-by machine, because at the time, in the

407
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:28,180
American glass industry, was a place full of child labor.

408
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:36,180
Before Owens, most bottles were made by hand-blowing gobs

409
00:28:36,280 --> 00:28:39,700
into molds, with all sorts of individual and idiosyncratic

410
00:28:39,800 --> 00:28:42,700
shapes. The Owens bottle machine,

411
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:46,740
weighing in at over 20 tons, sucked molten glass into

412
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:49,940
identical molds shaped like bottles.

413
00:28:49,941 --> 00:28:52,900
Other inventors refined the machine so that gravity

414
00:28:52,980 --> 00:28:56,680
replaced suction and the gob of glass fell into a mold.

415
00:28:56,740 --> 00:29:00,480
And what would have taken a day of labor to make bottles now

416
00:29:00,580 --> 00:29:03,180
can be accomplished in about seven minutes.

417
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:05,480
Bottles could now be made automatically

418
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:07,300
at a rate of 16 per minute.

419
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:15,000
The soda pop industry would never be the same.

420
00:29:15,001 --> 00:29:19,740
glass didn't just illuminate our lives.

421
00:29:19,840 --> 00:29:21,640
It changed the way we lived.

422
00:29:21,740 --> 00:29:24,240
Sealed bottles kept our food from spoiling.

423
00:29:24,340 --> 00:29:27,340
Light bulbs filled our homes with safe, clean light.

424
00:29:27,440 --> 00:29:30,340
Even our streets were lined with glass lamps.

425
00:29:30,440 --> 00:29:33,880
Next, we needed to see what was coming, because soon the streets

426
00:29:33,980 --> 00:29:37,060
would fill with automobiles, another great invention that

427
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:39,060
would benefit from the marvels of glass.

428
00:29:39,061 --> 00:29:46,060
of glass. Beverages should never be stored

429
00:29:46,061 --> 00:29:49,060
in leaded crystal, as toxic lead can leach into the liquid within

430
00:29:49,061 --> 00:29:54,060
days or even hours, depending on the crystal's lead content.

431
00:29:54,061 --> 00:30:00,060
Glass will return on Modern Marvels.

432
00:30:00,061 --> 00:30:07,060
We now return to Glass on Modern Marvels.

433
00:30:07,061 --> 00:30:09,060
The modern city is a city of glass.

434
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:23,060
Glass exteriors make up the skin of many skyscrapers, a concept

435
00:30:23,061 --> 00:30:27,060
that would have been shocking to architects of the 19th century,

436
00:30:27,061 --> 00:30:30,060
who built their skyscrapers of mortar and steel.

437
00:30:30,061 --> 00:30:33,060
But large, strong sheets of glass can be hung like a curtain on

438
00:30:33,061 --> 00:30:37,060
the steel frame of a building, creating a modern look from

439
00:30:37,061 --> 00:30:41,060
material. Glass now sheaths our tallest

440
00:30:41,061 --> 00:30:45,060
buildings because glass can be exceptionally strong, which is all

441
00:30:45,061 --> 00:30:48,060
the more surprising when you realize that during the early

442
00:30:48,061 --> 00:30:51,060
part of the last century, glass traffic lights were regularly

443
00:30:51,061 --> 00:30:58,060
shattered by snowflakes. There were railroad signal

444
00:30:58,061 --> 00:31:01,060
lights which were being made of standard sodium borosilicate

445
00:31:01,061 --> 00:31:06,060
types of glass. In the winter, you get snow landing

446
00:31:06,061 --> 00:31:08,700
landing on the outside of that glass.

447
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:12,360
But inside, you have a lantern or you have a candle in it,

448
00:31:12,460 --> 00:31:14,100
and so the inside is hot.

449
00:31:14,200 --> 00:31:19,300
When the snow hits the hot glass, it used to shatter.

450
00:31:19,400 --> 00:31:21,400
In their search for a better red light,

451
00:31:21,500 --> 00:31:24,540
Corning stumbled upon a glass made with boron that could

452
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:27,820
withstand extreme temperature variations.

453
00:31:27,920 --> 00:31:30,860
You can use many elements from the periodic table

454
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:33,520
and put them together and create a structure which

455
00:31:33,620 --> 00:31:34,760
is a glass.

456
00:31:34,761 --> 00:31:38,100
The glass is the characteristic that it is amorphous,

457
00:31:38,200 --> 00:31:42,260
inorganic material, which has generally transparency

458
00:31:42,360 --> 00:31:46,500
and typically very hard surface structure.

459
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:49,500
Corning named their new glass Pyrex.

460
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:51,200
You can take it right out of the freezer,

461
00:31:51,300 --> 00:31:53,920
and you can stick it in a 400-degree oven,

462
00:31:54,020 --> 00:31:55,620
and it doesn't shatter.

463
00:31:55,621 --> 00:32:06,020
shatter. Pyrex made scientific and

464
00:32:06,021 --> 00:32:10,140
medical research easier too. Pyrex could be used to conduct

465
00:32:10,141 --> 00:32:14,860
experiments involving temperature extremes without fear of explosion.

466
00:32:14,861 --> 00:32:18,220
And glass is ideal because it is inert.

467
00:32:18,221 --> 00:32:21,780
In other words, other substances aren't likely to break it down or

468
00:32:21,781 --> 00:32:23,140
bond with it.

469
00:32:23,240 --> 00:32:26,680
Glass is very, very stable, and you

470
00:32:26,780 --> 00:32:30,120
can put extremely caustic or harsh chemicals into a glass

471
00:32:30,220 --> 00:32:33,560
container or a glass channel or a glass tube,

472
00:32:33,660 --> 00:32:37,820
and it will have no effect or very little effect on the glass

473
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:42,100
relative to many other materials that you would use.

474
00:32:42,200 --> 00:32:45,240
By the mid-20th century, strong, thermally stable,

475
00:32:45,340 --> 00:32:48,140
and enormous pieces of glass similar to Pyrex could be

476
00:32:48,240 --> 00:32:51,780
manufactured and used as building exteriors.

477
00:32:51,781 --> 00:32:55,380
New buildings clad only in glass began to appear in cities like

478
00:32:55,480 --> 00:32:58,680
New York and Chicago. One of the first glass-curtain

479
00:32:58,780 --> 00:33:03,320
skyscrapers was New York's Lever House in 1952, headquarters

480
00:33:03,420 --> 00:33:07,320
of Lever Brothers, the soap manufacturer.

481
00:33:07,420 --> 00:33:11,440
As the glass gets larger and the structural system becomes less

482
00:33:11,540 --> 00:33:15,840
and less apparent, the buildings seem to get lighter and kind

483
00:33:15,940 --> 00:33:19,680
of more gossamer in their construction.

484
00:33:19,681 --> 00:33:22,580
These big glass sheets were further strengthened by

485
00:33:22,680 --> 00:33:26,480
tempering, a process of cooling related to annealing.

486
00:33:26,580 --> 00:33:29,120
They were often laminated to further protect them from

487
00:33:29,220 --> 00:33:33,620
abrasions that might cause structural weaknesses.

488
00:33:33,720 --> 00:33:35,860
Lamination would prove particularly useful in

489
00:33:35,960 --> 00:33:39,500
automobile glass, although cars are dependent on glass for

490
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:40,800
other reasons.

491
00:33:40,900 --> 00:33:43,940
Without glass, there would be no cars as we know them.

492
00:33:44,040 --> 00:33:47,680
It's interesting to note that the design of a modern car,

493
00:33:47,681 --> 00:33:50,680
the structural integrity of a modern car is dependent on the

494
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:54,180
glass that's in the car. The car would not survive the crash

495
00:33:54,280 --> 00:33:57,520
test were it not for the fact that the glass is part of the

496
00:33:57,620 --> 00:34:02,520
structural system of the car. The stiffness of the window

497
00:34:02,620 --> 00:34:05,940
panels helps the car retain its shape in a collision.

498
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:09,560
Better still, the lamina could also prevent broken glass from

499
00:34:09,660 --> 00:34:12,380
turning into dangerous missiles.

500
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:15,980
Autoglass is made to remain intact after accidents,

501
00:34:15,981 --> 00:34:17,980
Sandwiched between strong laminates,

502
00:34:17,981 --> 00:34:20,980
it is heat-treated to break into small pieces on impact,

503
00:34:20,981 --> 00:34:23,980
rather than shards, in a condition called dicing.

504
00:34:27,980 --> 00:34:30,980
Another revolutionary invention of the 20th century relied on

505
00:34:30,981 --> 00:34:36,980
glass to fully blossom. Television needed specially

506
00:34:36,981 --> 00:34:40,980
designed glass components called cathode ray picture tubes

507
00:34:40,981 --> 00:34:44,980
to project an image onto the glass TV screen.

508
00:34:44,981 --> 00:34:47,880
These large vacuum tubes are similar to light bulbs,

509
00:34:47,980 --> 00:34:50,880
only harder to make because of their size and shape.

510
00:34:50,980 --> 00:34:53,880
Corning scientist Jim Giffen simplified the process

511
00:34:53,980 --> 00:34:54,880
in the 1940s.

512
00:34:54,980 --> 00:34:57,420
He not only spun the earlier television bulbs,

513
00:34:57,520 --> 00:35:00,320
which were actually circular, but figured out a way to spin

514
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:02,640
glass into a rectangle, sort of like saying,

515
00:35:02,740 --> 00:35:06,440
I can figure out how to blow a rectangular soap bubble.

516
00:35:06,540 --> 00:35:08,340
Mass-produced glass picture tubes

517
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:10,640
helped make televisions more affordable.

518
00:35:10,740 --> 00:35:13,940
Living rooms have never been the same, nor have offices,

519
00:35:13,941 --> 00:35:16,840
as the same cathode ray tubes help computers to project

520
00:35:16,940 --> 00:35:19,940
their streams of information.

521
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:22,340
If a lot of these glass innovations seem to be coming

522
00:35:22,440 --> 00:35:24,480
from Corning, it's because they are.

523
00:35:24,580 --> 00:35:27,980
Most companies can't claim that they have made any innovations

524
00:35:28,080 --> 00:35:29,980
that have changed the lives of mankind.

525
00:35:30,080 --> 00:35:31,500
I mean, serious.

526
00:35:31,600 --> 00:35:33,900
Corning has been involved with three.

527
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:36,000
The light bulb, the picture tube,

528
00:35:36,100 --> 00:35:38,940
and fiber optic cable aren't flukes.

529
00:35:39,040 --> 00:35:41,840
Corning had a long-standing commitment to research.

530
00:35:41,841 --> 00:35:45,840
I think we were probably the third corporate research lab in the country.

531
00:35:45,841 --> 00:35:47,840
I think GE and Westinghouse were a little ahead of us,

532
00:35:47,841 --> 00:35:51,840
and I may be missing one or two others, but not many.

533
00:35:51,841 --> 00:35:54,840
And we started our laboratory in 1908.

534
00:35:54,841 --> 00:35:58,840
Corning invests what they call patient money,

535
00:35:58,841 --> 00:36:02,840
money they're willing to spend on research that may have no immediate application.

536
00:36:06,840 --> 00:36:10,840
It's a safe bet that many things glass scientists are working on now,

537
00:36:10,841 --> 00:36:14,080
you'll be using in 20 or 30 years.

538
00:36:14,081 --> 00:36:19,580
Next, what are glass scientists working on now?

539
00:36:19,581 --> 00:36:22,700
A float glass factory can produce sheet glass continuously

540
00:36:22,701 --> 00:36:27,780
for up to 12 years, over 50,000 miles of it.

541
00:36:27,781 --> 00:36:33,880
Glass will return on Modern Marvels.

542
00:36:33,881 --> 00:36:38,780
We now return to Glass on Modern Marvels.

543
00:36:38,781 --> 00:36:42,220
Within the last few years, a revival in hand-blown glass

544
00:36:42,320 --> 00:36:44,920
objects has produced an artistic renaissance in the

545
00:36:45,020 --> 00:36:46,520
glass art world.

546
00:36:46,620 --> 00:36:50,120
Just like our glass workshop in Indiana, hot shops are now

547
00:36:50,220 --> 00:36:53,320
producing all sorts of objects, from paperweights to

548
00:36:53,420 --> 00:36:55,220
margarita glasses.

549
00:36:55,320 --> 00:36:58,440
It's really a great material to just go in there and play

550
00:36:58,540 --> 00:37:01,340
with, and it's always fighting back.

551
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:02,640
It really has a personality.

552
00:37:02,740 --> 00:37:07,180
I think anyone that deals with glass, especially in its

553
00:37:07,181 --> 00:37:10,520
will tell you that it's a collaboration between you

554
00:37:10,620 --> 00:37:12,420
and the glass.

555
00:37:12,520 --> 00:37:15,480
Michael Amos and his colleagues are artists who express

556
00:37:15,580 --> 00:37:17,820
themselves through molten glass.

557
00:37:17,920 --> 00:37:20,920
They continue a tradition that includes some of the most

558
00:37:21,020 --> 00:37:24,840
breathtaking examples of painstaking creativity

559
00:37:28,340 --> 00:37:31,140
and exacting detail ever known.

560
00:37:31,141 --> 00:37:43,140
known, from highly detailed mosaics to scientifically

561
00:37:43,141 --> 00:37:46,140
exact reproductions, all in glass.

562
00:37:50,140 --> 00:37:52,140
Glass continues to have a significant role

563
00:37:52,141 --> 00:37:55,140
to play in man's artistic endeavors.

564
00:37:55,141 --> 00:37:58,140
But clearly, the future of glass isn't limited to aesthetic

565
00:37:58,141 --> 00:37:59,140
applications.

566
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:14,120
Scientists are already forging our future in liquid crystal displays and endoscopic laser

567
00:38:14,121 --> 00:38:15,580
surgery.

568
00:38:15,581 --> 00:38:22,260
If we all had the crystal ball, we could see what role glass is going to play in the

569
00:38:22,261 --> 00:38:23,260
future.

570
00:38:23,261 --> 00:38:28,820
I have a hard time thinking specifically of where it's going to be.

571
00:38:28,821 --> 00:38:31,320
I don't think anybody in the 1850s

572
00:38:31,321 --> 00:38:33,160
would have predicted where glass was going to be.

573
00:38:33,161 --> 00:38:36,160
And sometimes our own attempts at doing that

574
00:38:36,161 --> 00:38:39,260
are our greatest limitations.

575
00:38:39,261 --> 00:38:40,160
Take the space shuttle.

576
00:38:45,360 --> 00:38:47,100
Corning designed the windows.

577
00:38:47,101 --> 00:38:48,940
The windows on the space shuttle,

578
00:38:48,941 --> 00:38:51,380
in fact, in all manned spacecraft

579
00:38:51,381 --> 00:38:54,240
since the Mercury program, have been made

580
00:38:54,241 --> 00:38:56,580
of ultra-low expansion glass.

581
00:38:56,581 --> 00:39:00,020
that survives not only the temperatures in deep space where

582
00:39:00,021 --> 00:39:02,120
you're down into absolute zero.

583
00:39:02,121 --> 00:39:05,480
So you have to have glass that survives a wide range of

584
00:39:05,481 --> 00:39:10,280
temperatures and is also durable from a mechanical

585
00:39:10,281 --> 00:39:12,340
point of view.

586
00:39:12,341 --> 00:39:15,320
Telescopes first allowed us to see the universe.

587
00:39:15,321 --> 00:39:17,740
Now glass is helping us to get there.

588
00:39:17,741 --> 00:39:21,220
Glass is also improving the world of medicine, far beyond

589
00:39:21,221 --> 00:39:24,080
what any simple microscope could achieve.

590
00:39:24,081 --> 00:39:29,460
People are familiar with things like fiber optic probes that are used during operations,

591
00:39:29,461 --> 00:39:31,140
all types of endoscopes.

592
00:39:31,141 --> 00:39:33,480
That's sort of on the macro scale.

593
00:39:33,481 --> 00:39:38,240
But much of what's happening is that it's heading to miniaturization.

594
00:39:38,241 --> 00:39:43,460
You are starting to see small pieces of glass that are like barcodes and they can identify

595
00:39:43,461 --> 00:39:46,920
an individual biological target.

596
00:39:46,921 --> 00:39:51,580
You're beginning to see pieces of glass that are radioactive which can be inserted into

597
00:39:51,581 --> 00:39:52,580
the body.

598
00:39:52,581 --> 00:39:56,080
biological on them that attacks a certain cancer, and they will

599
00:39:56,081 --> 00:39:59,080
attach that cancer. Glass has made possible

600
00:39:59,081 --> 00:40:02,280
the mapping of the human genome. You actually have to put

601
00:40:02,281 --> 00:40:05,120
the DNA on something to conduct experiments.

602
00:40:05,121 --> 00:40:10,640
And we've been working for several years to develop a

603
00:40:10,641 --> 00:40:17,380
process to print consistently DNA on slides, up to 10,000

604
00:40:17,381 --> 00:40:21,080
dots of different DNA on each slide.

605
00:40:21,081 --> 00:40:23,820
So we could essentially print the entire human genome

606
00:40:23,821 --> 00:40:27,560
on two or three slides and make it available to scientists

607
00:40:27,561 --> 00:40:30,320
for their discovery process.

608
00:40:30,321 --> 00:40:34,040
Glass is going to allow you to wake up in the morning

609
00:40:34,041 --> 00:40:36,880
and just put a little drop of blood

610
00:40:36,881 --> 00:40:39,980
or a little drop of some other body fluid

611
00:40:39,981 --> 00:40:42,320
and send the information through a computer

612
00:40:42,321 --> 00:40:44,720
to be analyzed and get the results back

613
00:40:44,721 --> 00:40:47,620
and know exactly where you stand.

614
00:40:47,621 --> 00:40:49,880
Televisions and computers will benefit again

615
00:40:49,881 --> 00:40:54,280
technology, as liquid crystal displays or LCDs make possible

616
00:40:54,281 --> 00:40:58,280
even smaller, sleeker machines. Essentially, these are

617
00:40:58,281 --> 00:41:01,720
large chips. So you're looking through the

618
00:41:01,721 --> 00:41:06,920
glass, but the glass is also the medium on which transistors are

619
00:41:06,921 --> 00:41:09,640
deposited. And the way the transistors work

620
00:41:09,641 --> 00:41:13,240
is they stimulate a liquid material that's deposited

621
00:41:13,241 --> 00:41:16,780
between the two sheets of glass. Transistors will use

622
00:41:16,781 --> 00:41:20,180
electricity to control the liquid, affecting whether light is

623
00:41:20,181 --> 00:41:22,420
blocked or passes through colored pixels that are

624
00:41:22,421 --> 00:41:26,180
embedded on the front screen, creating the images you will see

625
00:41:26,181 --> 00:41:30,020
on your desktop or your flat screen TV.

626
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:33,220
From computer screens to weaponry, a glass ceramic

627
00:41:33,221 --> 00:41:37,340
hybrid is used in the nose cones of radar-guided missiles.

628
00:41:37,341 --> 00:41:41,180
Not only can these glass ceramic cones withstand heat, they are

629
00:41:41,181 --> 00:41:44,940
invisible to radar and therefore do not interfere with a radar

630
00:41:44,941 --> 00:41:50,940
guidance mechanism inside. Other glass applications endeavor

631
00:41:51,040 --> 00:41:54,580
to control its transparency, increasing or decreasing it

632
00:41:54,680 --> 00:41:57,580
depending on the situation. One of the limitations of

633
00:41:57,680 --> 00:42:04,900
ordinary glass, however, is that you can't make it change its

634
00:42:05,000 --> 00:42:08,400
light transmission characteristics when you want it to.

635
00:42:08,500 --> 00:42:13,740
Smart glass is a technology that we've invented and developed

636
00:42:13,741 --> 00:42:17,340
which makes this possible. Smart glass allows you to

637
00:42:17,341 --> 00:42:20,340
control the transmission of light through your window by

638
00:42:20,341 --> 00:42:22,840
turning a knob. In other words, a voltage

639
00:42:22,841 --> 00:42:25,880
control to regulate the transmission of your window

640
00:42:25,881 --> 00:42:29,280
between a dark state and a clear state, or you can tune it

641
00:42:29,281 --> 00:42:32,800
in to any degree you want in between those states.

642
00:42:32,801 --> 00:42:38,800
In addition, our windows will probably cut out more than 99%

643
00:42:38,801 --> 00:42:42,800
of the ultraviolet radiation that would normally impinge on

644
00:42:42,801 --> 00:42:46,600
on the window. Smart glass has a special film

645
00:42:46,601 --> 00:42:49,800
with a liquid suspension that, when electricity is applied,

646
00:42:49,801 --> 00:42:53,340
changes its opacity. Combined with other technology

647
00:42:53,341 --> 00:42:57,040
that reflects solar heat, smart glass could be a real energy

648
00:42:57,041 --> 00:43:02,960
saver, particularly in glass curtain skyscrapers.

649
00:43:02,961 --> 00:43:06,560
Best of all, glass is one of the most recyclable products on

650
00:43:06,561 --> 00:43:09,560
Earth. Recycled glass products reduce

651
00:43:09,561 --> 00:43:16,040
related air pollution by 20% and related water pollution by 50%.

652
00:43:16,041 --> 00:43:20,200
40% of previously used glass goes back into circulation every

653
00:43:20,201 --> 00:43:23,800
year. Glass is so amazing that scientists

654
00:43:23,801 --> 00:43:26,840
who have worked with glass for years still find themselves in

655
00:43:26,841 --> 00:43:31,240
all of it. I lead a group of scientists,

656
00:43:31,241 --> 00:43:34,520
the most creative glass research group in the world.

657
00:43:34,521 --> 00:43:39,160
It's an absolute delight to lead these challenging people who

658
00:43:39,161 --> 00:43:47,000
still maintain that inquisitiveness, that curiosity about what makes glass what it is.

659
00:43:47,001 --> 00:43:51,480
How can we deliver the next property, the next functionality, but that still maintain

660
00:43:51,481 --> 00:43:55,320
that fascination for glass as an art form as well.

661
00:43:55,321 --> 00:44:07,160
It is a surprising, mysterious, strange material that seems to have a mind of its own.

662
00:44:07,161 --> 00:44:09,960
And as a glass maker or a glass designer,

663
00:44:09,961 --> 00:44:13,660
as a glass technologist, you come to respect this material

664
00:44:13,661 --> 00:44:17,160
and mysteries that are locked inside of it.

665
00:44:17,161 --> 00:44:20,600
And you go to work every day sort of saying,

666
00:44:20,601 --> 00:44:21,600
can I figure it out?

667
00:44:21,601 --> 00:44:23,820
What's there waiting for me?

668
00:44:23,821 --> 00:44:26,020
Scientists continue to be challenged

669
00:44:26,021 --> 00:44:28,500
by the potential of glass.

670
00:44:28,501 --> 00:44:31,220
Meanwhile, we marvel at its usefulness,

671
00:44:31,221 --> 00:44:36,020
the ingenuity of its manufacture,

672
00:44:36,021 --> 00:44:39,020
the beauty of its creation.

